@NODE MAIN "Commodore BASIC 3.5 - Quick Guide"

@{b}Commodore BASIC 3.5 - Quick Guide@{ub}
For the Plus/4, C16 and C116 (xplus4).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  @{" Presentation      " LINK PRESENTATION}  BASIC 3.5 in a few words
  @{" Instructions      " LINK INSTRUCTIONS}  Every keyword, with examples
  @{" Video memory map  " LINK VIDEO}  Screen, colors, TED registers
  @{" Sound memory map  " LINK SOUND}  TED sound registers
  @{" Examples          " LINK EXAMPLES}  Six short programs to type

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@ENDNODE

@NODE PRESENTATION "Presentation"
@{b}Presentation@{ub}

BASIC 3.5 is the BASIC of the Plus/4, C16 and C116. It is the C64's
BASIC V2 plus about 50 keywords: graphics (GRAPHIC, DRAW, CIRCLE, BOX,
PAINT), sound (SOUND, VOL), structured loops (DO ... LOOP, WHILE,
UNTIL, EXIT), IF ... THEN ... ELSE, error trapping (TRAP, RESUME),
disk commands (DIRECTORY, DLOAD, DSAVE...) and editing tools (AUTO,
RENUMBER, DELETE, HELP, TRON). A V2 program usually runs, except its
POKEs: the chips and addresses are not the C64's.

A line typed with a number in front is stored in the program; a line
typed without a number runs at once (direct mode).

  10 PRINT "HELLO"       stores line 10
  RUN                    runs the program
  LIST                   shows it

@{b}Lines@{ub}: numbered 0 to 63999, several instructions separated by ":".

@{b}Variables@{ub}: only the first 2 characters of a name count. A is a
number, A% an integer (-32768 to 32767), A$ a string (255 characters).
Arrays with DIM. Reserved variables:
  TI, TI$   the clock (1/60 s, "HHMMSS")
  ST        status of the last input/output
  ER, EL    number and line of the last error (see TRAP)
  DS, DS$   disk status: number and message
  pi        the pi key: 3.14159265

@{b}Colors@{ub}: 16 colors, 1 to 16 in BASIC (see the video page), each
with 8 luminances, 0 (dark) to 7 (bright): 121 different colors.

@{b}Function keys@{ub}: F1-F7 and HELP type texts set by KEY. HELP shows
the line of the last error. RUN/STOP stops a program.

@{b}TEDMON@{ub}: MONITOR starts the machine code monitor (X to leave).
@ENDNODE

@NODE INSTRUCTIONS "Instructions"
@{b}Instructions@{ub}

The keywords new in BASIC 3.5 are marked (3.5).

@{b}ABS(X)@{ub}
  Absolute value of X.

@{b}AND@{ub}
  Logical and, also bit by bit on 16 bit integers.

@{b}ASC(X$)@{ub}
  PETSCII code of the first character of X$.

@{b}ATN(X)@{ub}
  Arc tangent of X, in radians.

@{b}AUTO N@{ub}  (3.5)
  Line numbers typed for you, step N. AUTO alone stops it.

@{b}BACKUP D0 TO D1@{ub}  (3.5)
  Copies a whole disk on a dual disk drive.

@{b}BOX C,X1,Y1,X2,Y2,A,P@{ub}  (3.5)
  Rectangle from X1,Y1 to X2,Y2 in color source C (0-3), turned by A
  degrees (optional), filled if P is 1 (optional).
  BOX 1,10,10,100,60,,1

@{b}CHAR C,X,Y,"TEXT",R@{ub}  (3.5)
  Prints TEXT at column X, row Y, also on a graphics screen. R=1 in
  reverse.  CHAR 1,10,5,"HELLO"

@{b}CHR$(X)@{ub}
  The character of PETSCII code X.  CHR$(147) clears the screen.

@{b}CIRCLE C,X,Y,XR,YR,SA,EA,A,I@{ub}  (3.5)
  Circle or ellipse at X,Y, radii XR and YR. Optional: start and end
  angles SA, EA, rotation A, degrees between segments I (I=120 makes a
  triangle).  CIRCLE 1,160,100,50,30

@{b}CLOSE N@{ub}
  Closes the file opened as N.

@{b}CLR@{ub}
  Clears all variables.

@{b}CMD N@{ub}
  Sends the output of PRINT to the open file N.

@{b}COLLECT D0@{ub}  (3.5)
  Validates a disk: frees the blocks of files not closed.

@{b}COLOR S,C,L@{ub}  (3.5)
  Sets the color of source S: 0 background, 1 foreground (drawing and
  text), 2 multicolor 1, 3 multicolor 2, 4 border. C color 1-16, L
  luminance 0-7.  COLOR 4,3,5  makes the border red.

@{b}CONT@{ub}
  Continues a stopped program.

@{b}COPY "OLD" TO "NEW"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Copies a file on the disk.

@{b}COS(X)@{ub}
  Cosine of X, in radians.

@{b}DATA@{ub}
  Values read by READ.

@{b}DEC("HEX")@{ub}  (3.5)
  The value of a hexadecimal number.  DEC("FF3E")  gives 65342.

@{b}DEF FN@{ub}
  Defines a function of one variable.  DEF FN SQ(X)=X*X

@{b}DELETE N-M@{ub}  (3.5)
  Deletes program lines N to M.  DELETE 100-200

@{b}DIM@{ub}
  Dimensions arrays.  DIM A(100),B$(10,10)

@{b}DIRECTORY@{ub}  (3.5)
  Lists the disk directory without losing the program in memory.
  DIRECTORY "A*" lists the files starting with A.

@{b}DLOAD "NAME"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Loads a BASIC program from disk drive 8.

@{b}DO / LOOP / WHILE / UNTIL / EXIT@{ub}  (3.5)
  Loop between DO and LOOP. DO WHILE C or LOOP UNTIL C ends it on a
  condition, EXIT leaves it at once.
  DO : GET K$ : LOOP UNTIL K$<>""

@{b}DRAW C,X1,Y1 TO X2,Y2 TO ...@{ub}  (3.5)
  Dot or lines in color source C.  DRAW 1,0,0 TO 319,199
  DRAW 1 TO X,Y continues from the last point.

@{b}DSAVE "NAME"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Saves the program on disk drive 8.

@{b}ELSE@{ub}  (3.5)
  What runs when the IF condition is false, after a ":".
  IF A>5 THEN PRINT "BIG" : ELSE PRINT "SMALL"

@{b}END@{ub}
  Ends the program.

@{b}ERR$(N)@{ub}  (3.5)
  The message of error number N.  PRINT ERR$(ER)

@{b}EXIT@{ub}  (3.5)
  Leaves a DO ... LOOP.

@{b}EXP(X)@{ub}
  e to the power X.

@{b}FN@{ub}
  Calls a function defined by DEF FN.

@{b}FOR ... TO ... STEP ... / NEXT@{ub}
  Counted loop.  FOR I=1 TO 10 STEP 2 : PRINT I : NEXT

@{b}FRE(0)@{ub}
  Free BASIC memory in bytes.

@{b}GET A$ / GETKEY A$@{ub}  (GETKEY 3.5)
  GET reads a key without waiting ("" if none), GETKEY waits for one.

@{b}GET# N, A$@{ub}
  Reads one character from the open file N.

@{b}GOSUB N / RETURN@{ub}
  Calls the subroutine at line N.

@{b}GOTO N@{ub}
  Jumps to line N.

@{b}GRAPHIC M,C@{ub}  (3.5)
  Screen mode M: 0 text, 1 high resolution 320x200, 2 high resolution
  with 5 text lines, 3 multicolor 160x200, 4 multicolor with 5 text
  lines. C=1 clears the bitmap. GRAPHIC CLR frees its memory.

@{b}GSHAPE A$,X,Y,M@{ub}  (3.5)
  Draws at X,Y a shape saved in A$ by SSHAPE. M: 0 as is, 1 inverted,
  2 OR, 3 AND, 4 XOR.

@{b}HEADER "NAME,ID",D0@{ub}  (3.5)
  Formats a disk (all its files are lost).

@{b}HELP@{ub}  (3.5)
  Lists the line where the last error happened, the error highlighted.

@{b}HEX$(N)@{ub}  (3.5)
  N (0-65535) as 4 hexadecimal digits.  HEX$(255)  gives "00FF".

@{b}IF ... THEN ... : ELSE ...@{ub}
  Runs the rest of the line when the condition is true, the ELSE part
  when it is false.

@{b}INPUT@{ub}
  Asks values.  INPUT "YOUR NAME";N$

@{b}INPUT# N@{ub}
  Reads values from the open file N.

@{b}INSTR(A$,B$,S)@{ub}  (3.5)
  Position of B$ in A$ (0 if absent), searched from S (optional).
  INSTR("COMMODORE","DO")  gives 6.

@{b}INT(X)@{ub}
  Integer part, rounded down.

@{b}JOY(N)@{ub}  (3.5)
  Joystick N (1 or 2): 0 centered, 1 up, 2 up right, 3 right, 4 down
  right, 5 down, 6 down left, 7 left, 8 up left, plus 128 with fire.

@{b}KEY N,"TEXT"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Sets function key N (1-8). CHR$(13) in TEXT types RETURN.
  KEY alone lists them.

@{b}LEFT$(X$,N)@{ub}
  The N first characters of X$.

@{b}LEN(X$)@{ub}
  Length of X$.

@{b}LET@{ub}
  Assignment, optional.

@{b}LIST@{ub}
  Shows the program. LIST 100-200.

@{b}LOAD "NAME",8@{ub}
  Loads a program from drive 8 (DLOAD is shorter).

@{b}LOCATE X,Y@{ub}  (3.5)
  Moves the graphics pixel cursor, where DRAW TO goes on.

@{b}LOG(X)@{ub}
  Natural logarithm of X.

@{b}MID$(X$,S,N)@{ub}
  N characters of X$ from position S.

@{b}MONITOR@{ub}  (3.5)
  Starts TEDMON, the machine code monitor. X goes back to BASIC.

@{b}NEW@{ub}
  Erases the program.

@{b}NOT@{ub}
  Logical not.

@{b}ON X GOTO / ON X GOSUB@{ub}
  Jumps to the Xth line number of the list.

@{b}OPEN N,D,S,"NAME"@{ub}
  Opens file N on device D.  OPEN 1,8,2,"DATA,S,W"

@{b}OR@{ub}
  Logical or, also bit by bit.

@{b}PAINT C,X,Y,M@{ub}  (3.5)
  Fills the area around X,Y with color source C. M=0: up to the color
  of C, M=1: up to any color but the background.

@{b}PEEK(A)@{ub}
  The byte at memory address A.

@{b}POKE A,V@{ub}
  Writes the byte V at address A.

@{b}POS(0)@{ub}
  The column of the cursor.

@{b}PRINT@{ub}
  Prints values. ? is a short PRINT.

@{b}PRINT USING "FORMAT";X@{ub}  (3.5)
  Formatted print: # a digit, . the decimal point, + - the sign, $ ...
  PRINT USING "####.##";3.14159   prints    3.14

@{b}PRINT# N@{ub}
  Prints to the open file N.

@{b}PUDEF " ,.$"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Changes the filler, comma, point and dollar characters of PRINT
  USING (European format: PUDEF " .,$").

@{b}RCLR(S)@{ub}  (3.5)
  The color of source S (see COLOR).

@{b}RDOT(N)@{ub}  (3.5)
  The pixel cursor: N=0 its X, 1 its Y, 2 the color source under it.

@{b}READ@{ub}
  Reads the next DATA values.

@{b}REM@{ub}
  A comment.

@{b}RENAME "OLD" TO "NEW"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Renames a file on the disk.

@{b}RENUMBER N,S,O@{ub}  (3.5)
  Renumbers the program from line O: new first line N, step S
  (default 10, 10, first line). GOTOs follow.

@{b}RESTORE@{ub}
  The next READ starts again at the first DATA.

@{b}RESUME@{ub}  (3.5)
  After a TRAP: RESUME runs the failing instruction again, RESUME NEXT
  the following one, RESUME N goes to line N.

@{b}RETURN@{ub}
  Ends a GOSUB subroutine.

@{b}RGR(0)@{ub}  (3.5)
  The current GRAPHIC mode.

@{b}RIGHT$(X$,N)@{ub}
  The N last characters of X$.

@{b}RLUM(S)@{ub}  (3.5)
  The luminance of color source S.

@{b}RND(X)@{ub}
  A random number from 0 to under 1. RND(-TI) once: different runs.

@{b}RUN@{ub}
  Runs the program.

@{b}SAVE "NAME",8@{ub}
  Saves the program (DSAVE is shorter).

@{b}SCALE N@{ub}  (3.5)
  SCALE 1: graphics coordinates 0-1023 on both axes, whatever the
  mode. SCALE 0: real pixels again.

@{b}SCNCLR@{ub}  (3.5)
  Clears the screen, text or graphics.

@{b}SCRATCH "NAME"@{ub}  (3.5)
  Deletes a file on the disk (asks ARE YOU SURE? in direct mode).

@{b}SGN(X)@{ub}
  Sign of X.

@{b}SIN(X)@{ub}
  Sine of X, in radians.

@{b}SOUND V,F,D@{ub}  (3.5)
  Plays on voice V (1, 2, or 3 for noise on voice 2) the frequency
  value F (0-1023, see the sound page) for D 60ths of a second. A new
  SOUND on a busy voice waits for the end of the previous one.

@{b}SPC(N)@{ub}
  In PRINT: N spaces.

@{b}SQR(X)@{ub}
  Square root of X.

@{b}SSHAPE A$,X1,Y1,X2,Y2@{ub}  (3.5)
  Saves the rectangle X1,Y1 - X2,Y2 of the graphics screen in A$, for
  GSHAPE.

@{b}STOP@{ub}
  Stops the program.

@{b}STR$(X)@{ub}
  The number X as a string.

@{b}SYS A@{ub}
  Runs machine code at address A.

@{b}TAB(N)@{ub}
  In PRINT: to column N.

@{b}TAN(X)@{ub}
  Tangent of X, in radians.

@{b}TRAP N@{ub}  (3.5)
  On an error, goes to line N instead of stopping: ER and EL tell
  which error and where, RESUME goes back. TRAP alone turns it off.

@{b}TRON / TROFF@{ub}  (3.5)
  Trace on: the number of each line run is printed. TROFF stops it.

@{b}USR(X)@{ub}
  Calls machine code, returns a value.

@{b}VAL(X$)@{ub}
  The number written in X$.

@{b}VERIFY "NAME",8@{ub}
  Compares the program in memory with the file.

@{b}VOL N@{ub}  (3.5)
  Sound volume, 0 to 8.

@{b}WAIT A,M,X@{ub}
  Waits until (PEEK(A) XOR X) AND M is not 0.
@ENDNODE

@NODE VIDEO "Video memory map"
@{b}Video memory map (Plus/4, C16)@{ub}

@{b}Screen and colors@{ub}
  Address        Hex          Use
  3072 - 4071    $0C00-$0FE7  screen: 40x25 screen codes
                              (row Y, column X: 3072+40*Y+X)
  2048 - 3047    $0800-$0BE7  attributes, one per character
                              (2048+40*Y+X): bits 0-3 color 0-15,
                              bits 4-6 luminance 0-7, bit 7 flashing
  4097           $1001        start of the BASIC program
  8192 - 16383   $2000-$3FFF  bitmap of GRAPHIC 1-4 (BASIC then
                              starts at 16385, $4001)

  An attribute value is color + 16 * luminance (+ 128 to flash), with
  the color 0-15: the BASIC color number minus 1.

@{b}TED registers@{ub} (65280 = $FF00)
  Address  Hex    Use
  65280    $FF00  timer 1, low and high byte (65280, 65281)
  65282    $FF02  timer 2 (65282, 65283)
  65284    $FF04  timer 3 (65284, 65285)
  65286    $FF06  control 1: bit 4 screen on, bit 5 bitmap mode,
                  bit 6 extended color, bit 3 25 rows,
                  bits 0-2 vertical scroll
  65287    $FF07  control 2: bit 4 multicolor, bit 3 40 columns,
                  bits 0-2 horizontal scroll, bit 6 NTSC
  65288    $FF08  keyboard latch
  65289    $FF09  interrupt flags
  65290    $FF0A  interrupt enable, bit 0 raster line bit 8
  65291    $FF0B  raster interrupt line
  65292    $FF0C  cursor position, high 2 bits
  65293    $FF0D  cursor position, low byte
  65298    $FF12  bits 3-5 bitmap address (8192 * n), bit 2 characters
                  from ROM (1) or RAM, bits 0-1 sound voice 1 high bits
  65299    $FF13  bits 2-7 character set address (1024 * n)
  65300    $FF14  bits 3-7 screen address (2048 * n): attributes there,
                  screen codes 1024 bytes after
  65301    $FF15  background color (color + 16 * luminance)
  65302    $FF16  background color 1 (multicolor, extended color)
  65303    $FF17  background color 2
  65304    $FF18  background color 3
  65305    $FF19  border color (color + 16 * luminance)
  65308    $FF1C  raster line, high bit
  65309    $FF1D  raster line, low byte
  65342    $FF3E  writing any value: ROM visible from 32768 up
  65343    $FF3F  writing any value: RAM visible from 32768 up

@{b}Colors@{ub} (BASIC numbers, COLOR; registers: number - 1)
  1 black       5 purple      9 orange      13 blue-green
  2 white       6 green      10 brown       14 light blue
  3 red         7 blue       11 yellow-green 15 dark blue
  4 cyan        8 yellow     12 pink        16 light green
  Luminance 0 (dark) to 7 (bright); color 1 (black) has none.
@ENDNODE

@NODE SOUND "Sound memory map"
@{b}Sound memory map (Plus/4, C16)@{ub}

The TED itself makes the sound: 2 square wave voices, voice 2 can make
noise instead. No envelope: a voice sounds while it is on.

  Address  Hex    Use
  65294    $FF0E  voice 1 frequency, low 8 bits
  65298    $FF12  bits 0-1: voice 1 frequency, high 2 bits (keep the
                  other bits: POKE 65298,(PEEK(65298) AND 252) OR H)
  65295    $FF0F  voice 2 frequency, low 8 bits
  65296    $FF10  bits 0-1: voice 2 frequency, high 2 bits
  65297    $FF11  bits 0-3 volume (0-8), bit 4 voice 1 on, bit 5 voice 2
                  on, bit 6 voice 2 noise, bit 7 D/A mode (sample play)

@{b}Frequency@{ub}: the same 10 bit value as BASIC's SOUND, 0-1023.
  value = 1024 - 110840 / Hz on a PAL machine
  value = 1024 - 111861 / Hz on an NTSC machine
  PAL octave 4:  C 600  D 647  E 688  F 707  G 741  A 772  B 800
                 C5 812

With BASIC, VOL and SOUND do all this:
  VOL 8 : SOUND 1,772,60    plays A (440 Hz) for one second.
  SOUND 3,900,30            half a second of noise.
@ENDNODE

@NODE EXAMPLES "Examples"
@{b}Examples@{ub}

Type them after NEW, or load them as .bas texts (BASIC menu, "Load
program as .bas"), then RUN. RUN/STOP stops them.

@{b}1. 121 colors@{ub}: every color and luminance in the border.

  10 FOR L=0 TO 7
  20 FOR C=1 TO 16
  30 COLOR 4,C,L
  40 FOR D=1 TO 100 : NEXT D
  50 NEXT C
  60 NEXT L
  70 PRINT "COLOR";RCLR(4);"LUMINANCE";RLUM(4)

@{b}2. Guess the number@{ub}: DO ... LOOP, IF ... ELSE.

  10 X=RND(-TI) : N=INT(RND(1)*100)+1 : T=0
  20 SCNCLR : PRINT "I THINK OF A NUMBER, 1 TO 100"
  30 DO
  40 INPUT "YOUR GUESS";G : T=T+1
  50 IF G<N THEN PRINT "TOO LOW" : ELSE IF G>N THEN PRINT "TOO HIGH"
  60 LOOP UNTIL G=N
  70 PRINT "FOUND IN";T;"TRIES"

@{b}3. Circles@{ub}: high resolution graphics.

  10 COLOR 0,2,7 : COLOR 1,7,3
  20 GRAPHIC 1,1
  30 FOR R=10 TO 90 STEP 10
  40 CIRCLE 1,160,100,R*1.5,R
  50 NEXT R
  60 BOX 1,0,0,319,199
  70 CHAR 1,1,1,"PRESS A KEY"
  80 GETKEY K$ : GRAPHIC 0

@{b}4. A house@{ub}: DRAW, BOX and PAINT.

  10 COLOR 0,2,7 : COLOR 1,3,4
  20 GRAPHIC 1,1
  30 BOX 1,100,100,220,180
  40 DRAW 1,100,100 TO 160,50 TO 220,100
  50 PAINT 1,160,80
  60 BOX 1,145,140,175,180,,1
  70 CIRCLE 1,270,40,20 : PAINT 1,270,40
  80 GETKEY K$ : GRAPHIC 0

@{b}5. A scale@{ub}: SOUND, PAL frequency values.

  10 VOL 8
  20 FOR N=1 TO 8
  30 READ F
  40 SOUND 1,F,20
  50 NEXT N
  60 SOUND 3,900,30
  70 DATA 600,647,688,707,741,772,800,812

@{b}6. Joystick and errors@{ub}: a ball moved with the joystick in port 2,
fire changes the border; TRAP catches the errors.

  10 TRAP 200 : SCNCLR : X=20 : Y=12
  20 DO
  30 CHAR 1,X,Y,"O"
  40 DO : J=JOY(2) : LOOP WHILE J=0
  50 CHAR 1,X,Y," "
  60 IF J AND 128 THEN COLOR 4,INT(RND(1)*16)+1,5 : J=J AND 127
  70 IF J=1 OR J=2 OR J=8 THEN Y=Y-1
  80 IF J>=4 AND J<=6 THEN Y=Y+1
  90 IF J>=2 AND J<=4 THEN X=X+1
  100 IF J>=6 AND J<=8 THEN X=X-1
  110 X=X-(X<0)+(X>39) : Y=Y-(Y<0)+(Y>24)
  120 FOR D=1 TO 30 : NEXT D
  130 LOOP
  200 PRINT ERR$(ER);" IN";EL : END

  Line 110: a true comparison is -1 in BASIC, so X=X-(X<0) adds 1
  when X is below 0, and +(X>39) takes 1 when X is above 39.
@ENDNODE
